## Use a Repeatable Workflow Every Time

When you need to do the same kind of work more than once, you should stop relying on memory. A simple workflow gives you the same starting point each time, cuts down on missed steps, and makes it easier to see where things went wrong.

{{< steps >}}
{{< step "Start With The Goal" >}}
Write down the outcome in one sentence. Keep it concrete. If you cannot say what done looks like, the rest of the workflow will drift.
{{< /step >}}
{{< step "List The Inputs" >}}
Gather the files, commands, notes, or links you need before you begin. This is the step that saves you from stopping halfway through to hunt for one missing detail.
{{< /step >}}
{{< step "Run The Same Core Actions" >}}
Do the work in the same order each time. For example: review the current state, make one focused change, then verify the result. That sequence stays stable even when the details change.
{{< /step >}}
{{< step "Capture What Changed" >}}
Leave a short note about what you did, what failed, and what you would repeat next time. A workflow gets better when you treat each run as input for the next one.
{{< /step >}}
{{< /steps >}}

This structure works because it removes avoidable decisions. You still have to think, but you spend that effort on the work itself instead of on reassembling your process from scratch.

The next part matters just as much: once you have a workflow, you need a way to verify that it actually produces the result you expect.

# Entity Access Policies

[Waaseyaa](./) uses access policies to decide who can view, create, update, or delete an entity.

## Install The Package

```bash
composer require waaseyaa/entity-access
```

This installs the entity access package in your project. You can register policies for any entity type after Composer updates your autoloader.

## Register Your Policy

```php
<?php

use App\Entities\Project;
use App\Policies\ProjectPolicy;
use Waaseyaa\EntityAccess\PolicyRegistry;

return static function (PolicyRegistry $policies): void {
    $policies->for(Project::class, ProjectPolicy::class);
};
```

This maps the `Project` entity to its policy class. When you load or mutate a project, the framework asks that policy for a decision.

## Define Your Entity Rules

```php
<?php

namespace App\Policies;

use App\Entities\Project;
use App\Models\User;
use Waaseyaa\EntityAccess\AccessResult;

final class ProjectPolicy
{
    public function view(User $user, Project $project): AccessResult
    {
        if ($project->is_public) {
            return AccessResult::allow();
        }

        return $project->owner_id === $user->id
            ? AccessResult::allow()
            : AccessResult::deny('You do not own this project.');
    }

    public function update(User $user, Project $project): AccessResult
    {
        return $project->owner_id === $user->id
            ? AccessResult::allow()
            : AccessResult::deny('Only the owner can update this project.');
    }
}
```

This policy keeps the rules close to the entity behavior. You return an explicit allow or deny result for each operation.

## Check Access Before You Save

```php
<?php

use App\Entities\Project;
use Waaseyaa\EntityAccess\AuthorizesEntities;

final class UpdateProjectAction
{
    use AuthorizesEntities;

    public function __invoke(Project $project, array $input): Project
    {
        $this->authorize('update', $project);

        $project->fill($input);
        $project->save();

        return $project;
    }
}
```

This stops the write before the entity changes. If the policy denies access, the action throws an authorization exception.

## Return A Clear HTTP Response

```php
<?php

use App\Entities\Project;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\JsonResponse;

final class ShowProjectController
{
    public function __invoke(Project $project): JsonResponse
    {
        abort_unless(auth()->user()->can('view', $project), 403);

        return new JsonResponse($project->toArray());
    }
}
```

This checks the policy at the edge of the request. Your controller stays small because the rule already lives in the policy.

## Test The Policy Behavior

```php
<?php

use App\Entities\Project;
use App\Models\User;
use App\Policies\ProjectPolicy;

it('allows owners to update their project', function (): void {
    $owner = User::factory()->make(['id' => 10]);
    $project = Project::factory()->make(['owner_id' => 10]);

    $result = (new ProjectPolicy())->update($owner, $project);

    expect($result->allowed())->toBeTrue();
});
```

This test covers the rule that matters most: ownership. Add matching tests for denied access so policy changes fail fast.

## Apply Policies Across Your Entity Layer

You can register a policy for each entity type.

Keep the rule names predictable: `view`, `create`, `update`, `delete`.

Write field-level checks only when the entity-level rule is too broad.

If a policy grows into a long chain of conditions, move shared checks into small methods. You keep the public methods readable, and you make failures easier to trace.
